  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 240 PM CST Fri Nov 13 2026\n\nA large slow moving upper level low over the upper Ohio river \nvalley (535 decameter 500 mb low nw PA), has a strong short \nwave trof extending westward into southern WI and northern IL.\nThis short wave trof was still producing isolated south bound \nlight rain showers in east central IL mixed at times with light\nsnow like recently at BMI. But trends have been diminishing and\nHRRR and RRFS models dissipate these by sunset. Nearly overcast\nlow clouds over east central and southeast IL and from\nJacksonville sw while scattered to broken diurnally driven\ncu/stratocumulus clouds over the IL river valley early this \nafternoon. Drier air working in from the NNW will see these \nclouds decrease from the north into this evening, lingering \nlongest over Vermilion county where a north/south oriented lake \nenhanced band of rain/snow showers will be closest to. With \nclouds and North to NW winds decreasing this evening, expect a \nour 1st widespread hard freeze over CWA overnight with lows in \nthe mid to upper 20s. \n\nSome low clouds may linger over Vermilion and Edgar counties\nSaturday, otherwise much of CWA will enjoy ample sunshine\nSaturday with lighter northwest winds as high pressure over\nMN/IA settles closer to IL. Another hard freeze likely Sat night\nwith high pressure nearby with clear skies and light to calm\nwinds, with lows in the mid 20s. High pressure to be over IL on\nSunday with sunny skies and light/calm winds with highs in the\nlower 50s, and lows in the upper 20s Sunday night. High pressure\nslowly moves away from IL across the Ohio river valley on Monday\nand into the central Appalachians Tue and continues a fair\namount of sunshine with highs in the low to mid 50s Monday and\nmid to upper 50s Tue. \n\nUpper level ridge will shift into the Southeast States with \ndeepening upper level trof into the Western States Wed-Fri with \nIL getting in a more unsettled SW upper level flow. Large \nsurface high pressure ridge over the mid Atlantic States Wed and\nWed night should still keep IL dry with SSE flow starting a \nwarm up. Highs Wed in the upper 50s/lower 60s climb into the mid\n60s to near 70F next Friday and Saturday (Nov 20-21). A weak \ndisturbance ejecting ne into MO/IL Thu could bring next chance \nof rain showers, especially over the IL river valley. A stronger\nsystem looks to arrive next weekend bringing in a better chance\nof convection. \n\nThe Climate Prediction Center (CPC) 8-14 Day Outlook for Nov\n21-27th has a 50-55% chance of above normal temperatures over \ncentral and southeast IL, and 35-40% chance of above normal \nprecipitation. So not very good chances of accumulating snows \nover the area during the next two weeks. CPC Week 3-4 Outlook\nfor Nov 28-Dec 11 (just released) has a 40-45% chance of above\nnormal temperatures over CWA, and 33-40% chance above normal\nprecipitation over central/southern IL and equal chances above\nand below normal precipitation over northern third of IL.",
